@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+---
+title: "sessions.py"
+date: 2026-09-23
+hash: "sha256:ccb0af70ec4c4e3e6d5d8328bca9aa82c482e3b01520987184231ff0dac3cd18"
+---
+## Decisions
+- On a session with a default, an explicit per-call `timeout=None` inherits the session default,
+  like `stream`/`verify`/`cert` do through `merge_setting`. The product owner delegated this choice
+  (2026-09-23). The deciding case: a helper that forwards its own `timeout=None` would otherwise
+  bypass the default and hang, which is the failure the feature exists to prevent. This rules out a
+  sentinel default in the `request()`/verb signatures that tells "omitted" from "None".
+- The effective timeout is resolved once, in `Session.send`, not in `request()` or
+  `merge_environment_settings`. `send` is the one path shared by the verb helpers, `request()`,
+  direct `send(prepared)`, following `response.next`, and every redirect hop. `resolve_redirects`
+  calls `self.send` with the already-resolved kwargs. This rules out adding `timeout` to
+  `merge_environment_settings`, whose return shape subclasses may depend on and which direct
+  `send()` never reaches.
+
+## Discussions
+- Setting-time validation of `Session.timeout` was considered and not added. No other `Session`
+  setting is validated on assignment. A malformed default fails at send time with the same
+  `ValueError` as a malformed per-call timeout, so it never silently becomes "no timeout".
